      "id": 1,
      "text": "Don Mueang opened as a Royal Thai Air Force base on 27 March 1914, becoming Thailand’s second airfield after Sra Pathum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Mueang_International_Airport",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first commercial flight to Don Mueang happened in 1924, marking it as one of the world’s earliest commercial aviation hubs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Mueang_International_Airport",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After Suvarnabhumi’s capacity limits, Don Mueang reopened on 24 March 2007 following extensive renovations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Mueang_International_Airport",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Since reopening, Don Mueang has become the de facto low‑cost carrier hub of Thailand and Southeast Asia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Mueang_International_Airport",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Terminal 3, the old domestic terminal, has been abandoned since 2011, reflecting the airport’s shift to low‑cost operations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
